{"records":[{"id":"3fjsc6y4n4","title":"Immunotherapy for EGFR Mutated Lung Cancer","description":"Patients with non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) harboring epidermal growth factor receptor ‎‎(EGFR) mutations face poor outcomes after progression on tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKIs). ‎The efficacy of immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) combined with chemotherapy in these ‎patients remains uncertain. ‎Therefore, we performed this meta-analysis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-07-08T16:34:10.35Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3fjsc6y4n4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3fjsc6y4n4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ezzeldin","last_name":"Ibrahim","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Meta-Analysis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hx5jtyxtxr","title":"Increased lactate secretion by cancer cells sustains non-cell-autonomous adaptive resistance to MET and EGFR targeted therapies. Apicella et al.","description":"Microenvironment is known to influence cancer drug response and sustain resistance to therapies targeting receptor-tyrosine kinases. However if and how tumor microenvironment can be altered during treatment, contributing to resistance onset is not known. Here we show that, under prolonged treatment with tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKIs), EGFR- or MET-addicted cancer cells displayed a metabolic shift towards increased glycolysis and lactate production. We identified secreted lactate as the key molecule able to instruct Cancer Associated Fibroblasts (CAFs) to produce Hepatocyte Growth Factor (HGF) in a NF-KB dependent manner. Increased HGF, activating MET-dependent signaling in cancer cells, sustained resistance to TKIs. Functional or pharmacological targeting of molecules involved in the lactate axis, such as lactate dehydrogenase or the lactate transporters MCT4 and MCT1, abrogated in vivo resistance, demonstrating the crucial role of this metabolite in the adaptive process. \nThis non-cell-autonomous, adaptive resistance mechanism was observed in NSCLC patients progressed on EGFR TKIs, demonstrating the clinical relevance of our findings and opening novel scenarios in the challenge to drug resistance.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-08-20T07:35:09.484Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hx5jtyxtxr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hx5jtyxtxr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Maria","last_name":"Apicella","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Elisa ","last_name":"Giannoni","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stephany","last_name":"Fiore","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Karin","last_name":"Ferrari","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Fernández-Pérez ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Claudio","last_name":"Isella","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Carlotta","last_name":"Granchi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Filippo","last_name":"Minutolo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Antonino","last_name":"Sottile","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Paolo","last_name":"Comoglio","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Enzo","last_name":"Medico","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Filippo","last_name":"Pietrantonio","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marco","last_name":"Volante","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Diego","last_name":"Pasini","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Paola","last_name":"Chiarugi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Silvia","last_name":"Giordano","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Simona ","last_name":"Corso","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mechanism of Resistance","Molecular Mechanism of Cancer","Translational Oncology"],"journal_issn":["15504131"]},{"id":"v6cszczy48","title":"Kinase Inhibitors Screen Tamoxifen Resistant Breast Cancer","description":"Antiestrogen resistance of breast cancer has been related to enhanced growth factor receptor expression and activation. We have previously shown that ectopic expression and subsequent activation of the insulin-like growth factor-1 receptor (IGF1R) or the epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) in MCF7 or T47D breast cancer cells results in antiestrogen resistance. In order to identify critical therapeutic targets that mediate this antiestrogen resistance, we performed a kinase inhibitor screen with 273 different inhibitors. Various ALK inhibitors, including TEA684, AZD3463 and LDK378, inhibited cell proliferation in IGF1R expressing cells under both normal and antiestrogen resistance conditions by preventing IGF1R activation and subsequent downstream signaling; ALK inhibitors did not affect EGFR signaling. On the other hand, MEK1/2 inhibitors, including PD0325901, selumetinib, trametinib and TAK733, selectively antagonized IGF1R signaling-mediated antiestrogen resistance but did not affect cell proliferation under normal growth conditions. The MEK inhibitors PD0325901, selumetinib, trametinib and TAK733 caused an arrest in the G0/G1 cell cycle phase only under antiestrogen resistance conditions in IGF1R expressing cells, but not under normal growth conditions. RNAseq analysis revealed that MEK inhibitors PD0325901 and selumetinib drastically altered cell cycle progression and cell migration networks under IGF1R signaling-mediated antiestrogen resistance. In a group of 291 patients with primary operable ER+ breast cancer, strong pMEK staining in metastatic breast cancer after first-line tamoxifen treatment was significantly related to no clinical benefit. We propose a critical role for MEK activation in IGF1R signaling-mediated antiestrogen resistance and anticipate that dual-targeted therapy with MEK inhibitor and antiestrogen could improve treatment outcome.  ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-06-06T16:25:15.469Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/v6cszczy48","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/v6cszczy48"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"John","last_name":"Meerman","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Breast Cancer","Estrogen","Tyrosine Kinase Inhibitor","Tamoxifen"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"53wrc86y6s","title":"Therapeutic application of extracellular vesicular EGFR isoform D as a co-drug to target squamous cell cancers with tyrosine kinase inhibitors","description":"Supplementary Information for manuscript \"Therapeutic application of extracellular vesicular EGFR isoform D as a co-drug to target squamous cell cancers with tyrosine kinase inhibitors\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-07-03T00:11:09.81Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/53wrc86y6s","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/53wrc86y6s"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Darren","last_name":"Toh","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hui sun","last_name":"Leong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fui Teen","last_name":"Chong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dorival Mendes - Junior","last_name":"Rodrigues","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Meng Jie","last_name":"Ren","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xue Lin ","last_name":"Kwang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dawn P X","last_name":"Lau","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Po-Hsien","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andre Luiz","last_name":"Vettore","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bin Tean","last_name":"Teh","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daniel S W","last_name":"Tan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"N Gopalakrishna","last_name":"Iyer","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Receptor Trafficking","Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor","Tyrosine Kinase Inhibitor","Head and Neck Cancer","Extracellular Vesicle"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"m9d29fhmc3","title":"METABOLOMIC DATA OF EGFR-TKI-SENSITIVE CELLS VS. EGFR-TKI-RESISTANCE CELLS","description":"Epider growth factor receptor-tyrosine kinase inhibitor (EGFR-TKI) acquired-resistance remains unknown. We determined a metabolomics profiling comparing EGFR-TKI-resistant cells to EGFR-TKI-sensitive cells using UPLC-QTOF-MS.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-07-12T10:08:35.682Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/m9d29fhmc3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/m9d29fhmc3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yu-Fei","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":"0000-0003-2475-570X"},{"first_name":"Ke-Ren","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ya-Bin","last_name":"Tang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Liang","last_name":"Zhu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Oncology","Metabolomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"55v76yw529","title":"Deubiquitinases: Pro-oncogenic Activity and Therapeutic Targeting in Acute Leukemia","description":"Dysregulation of kinase signaling pathways via mutations favors tumor cell survival and resistance to therapy and it is common in cancer. In our work we reveal a novel mechanism of posttranslational regulation of kinase signaling and nuclear receptor activity via deubiquitination in acute leukemia. We observed that the ubiquitin specific protease 7 (USP7) forms a complex with USP11 to deubiquitinate oncogenic lymphocyte cell-specific protein tyrosine kinase (LCK). Deubiquitination of LCK controls its activity, thereby altering T cell receptor signaling. Impairment of LCK activity leads to increased expression of the glucocorticoid receptor (GR) transcript, culminating into transcriptional activation of pro-apoptotic target genes and sensitizes cells to glucocorticoids in primary T cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (T-ALL) patient samples. The transcriptional activation of pro-apoptotic target genes, such as BCL2L11, is orchestrated by the deubiquitinase activity and mediated via an increase in enhancer-promoter interaction intensity. Our data unveil how dysregulated deubiquitination controls signaling pathways, leading to cancer cell survival and drug non-response, and suggest novel therapeutic combinations towards targeting leukemia.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-16T20:51:22.317Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/55v76yw529","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/55v76yw529"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Blanca","last_name":"Gutierrez","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Protein-Tyrosine Kinase","Ubiquitylation","Acute Leukemia"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hts8z9mx4w","title":"OptiTROP-Lung03 Trial Part II Data","description":"Part II of the OptiTROP-Lung03 trial (NCT05631262) is a phase 2, open label, randomised controlled trial to compare sac-TMT with docetaxel for EGFR-mutated locally advanced or metastatic NSCLC with disease progression after previous treatment with EGFR-tyrosine kinase inhibitors and platinum based chemotherapy.\nTumour assessments were conducted according to the RECIST (response evaluation criteria in solid tumours) version 1.1, with evaluations performed every six weeks during the first 48 weeks after randomisation and every 12 weeks thereafter. The primary endpoint was BIRC assessed objective response rate, defined as the proportion of patients with a confirmed complete response or partial response as the best overall response. \nThis multicentre, randomised controlled trial found statistically significant improvements in objective response rate, progression-free survival, and overall survival with sac-TMT compared with docetaxel in patients with EGFR-mutated locally advanced or metastatic NSCLC, with a manageable safety profile. This study provides evidence supporting sac-TMT as an effective treatment for these patients with disease progression after treatment with EGFR-tyrosine kinase inhibitors and platinum based chemotherapy. \nThe dataset includes: ID data of patients，random date, baseline ECOG score, histology, clinical stage, random group, last alive date, date of death, best confirmed efficacy assessed by BIRC , adverse events related to the investigational drug, drug-related serious adverse events, and PT terms for adverse events of grade 3 or higher related to the study drug.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-28T11:14:18.22Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hts8z9mx4w","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hts8z9mx4w"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Li","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"Sichuan Kelun-Biotech Biopharmaceutical Co., Ltd.","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer","Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor","Mutation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y2y4wmn8dz","title":"OptiTROP-Lung03 Trial Part II Data","description":"Part II of the OptiTROP-Lung03 trial (NCT05631262) is a phase 2, open label, randomised controlled trial to compare sac-TMT with docetaxel for EGFR-mutated locally advanced or metastatic NSCLC with disease progression after previous treatment with EGFR-tyrosine kinase inhibitors and platinum based chemotherapy.\nTumour assessments were conducted according to the RECIST (response evaluation criteria in solid tumours) version 1.1, with evaluations performed every six weeks during the first 48 weeks after randomisation and every 12 weeks thereafter. The primary endpoint was BIRC assessed objective response rate, defined as the proportion of patients with a confirmed complete response or partial response as the best overall response. \nThis multicentre, randomised controlled trial found statistically significant improvements in objective response rate, progression-free survival, and overall survival with sac-TMT compared with docetaxel in patients with EGFR-mutated locally advanced or metastatic NSCLC, with a manageable safety profile. This study provides evidence supporting sac-TMT as an effective treatment for these patients with disease progression after treatment with EGFR-tyrosine kinase inhibitors and platinum based chemotherapy. \nThe dataset includes: ID data of patients, random date, baseline ECOG score, histology, clinical stage, random group, last alive date, date of death, best confirmed efficacy assessed by BIRC, adverse events related to the investigational drug, drug-related serious adverse events, and PT terms for adverse events of grade 3 or higher related to the study drug.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-07-23T22:55:26.004Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y2y4wmn8dz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y2y4wmn8dz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Li","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"Sichuan Kelun-Biotech Biopharmaceutical Co., Ltd.","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer","Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor","Mutation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cs75tx8gsr","title":"Simulation of Cancer Metabolism","description":"The original data from the manuscript \"Computational analysis of receptor tyrosine kinase inhibitors and cancer metabolism: Implications for treatment and discovery of potential therapeutic signatures\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-06-16T13:46:52.903Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cs75tx8gsr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cs75tx8gsr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jian","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer Treatment","Computer Simulation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"jynzg6jdjz","title":"Towards identification of novel inhibitors of EGFR mutants through In- silico approach.","description":"This study employed molecular docking techniques to identify potential inhibitors against wild-type EGFR and its clinically relevant mutations, including the exon 19 deletion and T790M/L858R resistance mutations. Nine compounds, comprising five irreversible tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKIs) and four small molecule natural compounds, were systematically screened using CB-dock2 computational tool. The drug-likeness and toxicity of these molecules were also examined based on their ADMET and Toxicity Prediction profiles. Among the tested compounds, Tetrandrine, Dauricine, and Olmutinib exhibited robust binding affinities across both wild-type and mutant EGFR configurations, highlighting their potential as effective inhibitors. These findings align with existing literature, reinforcing the importance of natural compounds and targeted inhibitors in combating EGFR-driven cancers. The integrated approach of combining molecular docking using CB-dock2, ADMET profiling, and Lipinski's rule of five provides a robust framework for preliminary drug candidate screening, potentially accelerating the development of more precise and effective EGFR-targeted therapies. The findings contribute to the growing body of research exploring alternative and more nuanced strategies for inhibiting EGFR-driven oncogenic mechanisms, highlighting the importance of computational methods in identifying novel molecular targets with improved specificity and reduced side effects. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-12-09T21:03:07.035Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jynzg6jdjz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jynzg6jdjz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Pallavi","last_name":"Khodlan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rajdeep ","last_name":"Chakraborty ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Priyanka","last_name":"Rajbhar","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Praveen ","last_name":"Kumar","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Raj ","last_name":"Kumar","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anil","last_name":"Kumar","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/0492wrx28","name":"Indian Council of Medical Research","grant_id":"(5/4/2-8/OH/2023-NCD-II) "}],"categories":["Bioinformatics","Docking"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":137397,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":185,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":137397,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":136796,"restricted":65,"embargoed":536},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2319},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1526},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":665},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":475},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":437},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":423},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":412},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":379},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":315},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":280}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":134752},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1904},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":187},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":136},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":76},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":58},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-